# Volume 02 — Raaste (Roads)
# rule|consequence|story_core|cast|place|breach_reason|twist|final_image|family|tone
# Bus stops
Never wait alone after the last bus|The stop keeps a seat for you|A night-shift worker waits for a bus that never comes; in the morning the bench has a fresh imprint and her bag is still there.|NIGHT WORKER; CLEANER; THE BUS|A bus stop with one working tube light|She had no other way home|The bus came at 4 AM and only she could see it|The empty bench, the bag, the waking street|threshold|eerie
Don't sit on the bench with the worn name|The name becomes yours|A student sits on a bench where someone carved a name; the next day his friends call him by that name, and he answers.|STUDENT; FRIEND; THE NAME|A bus stop bench with a carved name|It was the only seat left|The name belongs to a boy who disappeared at the stop|The carved name, now freshly cut, the student's hand|mimicry|eerie
Never ask a stranger when the next bus is coming|They tell you your own schedule|A woman asks a stranger for the bus time; he tells her exactly when she will leave her house, and when she will stop coming back.|WOMAN; STRANGER; BUS CONDUCTOR|A bus stop in the rain|She was late for work|The stranger is her future self, waiting for the same bus|The timetable, the woman's handwriting, the empty road|departure|eerie
Don't leave your bag on the seat to save it|Someone sits in the bag|A man leaves his bag on the seat while he buys tea; he returns to find the bag upright and a small wet shape inside it.|MAN; TEA SELLER; THE PASSENGER|A bus stop beside a tea stall|He didn't want to lose his seat|The shape is a child who died waiting for the bus|The bag, the wet seat, the man's tea going cold|contamination|sad
Never board a bus that has no lights|It takes you to the stop you missed|A boy boards a dark bus at dusk; the conductor collects his fare, and the bus stops at a village he left years ago.|BOY; CONDUCTOR; THE DRIVER|A bus stop at the edge of the city|He wanted to get home faster|The village was demolished before he was born|The dark bus, the boy's old house, the conductor's hand|departure|eerie
Don't count the people at the stop|The count includes you twice|A woman counts the people waiting at the stop; every time she counts, there is one more person, and they are all facing her.|WOMAN; OLD MAN; THE CROWD|A crowded bus stop at rush hour|She was bored|The extra person is her, standing behind her|The crowd, the woman's back, the bus arriving|counting|eerie
Never accept tea from a bus stop vendor after midnight|The tea is always someone's last|A traveler accepts tea from a vendor at midnight; the tea is warm and sweet, and the vendor tells him it was the last thing a man drank before he died.|TRAVELER; VENDOR; THE MAN|A tea stall beside a bus stop|He was cold and tired|The man who died is the traveler, in a future he can't remember|The cup, the vendor's smile, the empty road|debt|eerie
Don't run after a bus that didn't stop|It was stopping for someone else|A girl runs after a bus that doesn't stop; it stops at the next corner, and the conductor opens the door for someone she can't see.|GIRL; CONDUCTOR; THE PASSENGER|A road outside a bus depot|She was late for an exam|The passenger is the girl, but older and already on the bus|The bus, the girl's reflection in the window, the empty seat|mimicry|eerie
Never wait at a stop that isn't on the map|The bus that comes is from another route|A man waits at a stop he finds on an old map; the bus that arrives has no number, and the passengers are all wearing his clothes.|MAN; DRIVER; THE PASSENGERS|A dirt road with an old concrete stop|He thought it was a shortcut|The bus is going to his own funeral|The stop, the bus, the man's coat on a passenger|departure|eerie
Don't leave an umbrella on the bench|It rains only where you stand|A woman leaves her umbrella on a bus stop bench; for a week it rains only on her, and the bench stays dry.|WOMAN; CLEANER; THE UMBRELLA|A bus stop on a sunny day|She didn't need it anymore|The umbrella was keeping someone else dry|The wet woman, the dry bench, the open umbrella|contamination|eerie
# Rickshaws
Never take a rickshaw that already has a passenger|The passenger is your reflection|A man hails a rickshaw with someone already in the back; the driver says the seat is empty, and the passenger turns to show the man's face.|MAN; DRIVER; THE PASSENGER|A rickshaw stand outside a market|He was in a hurry|The passenger is the man's reflection, already at his destination|The rickshaw, the mirror, the man's face in the back|reflection|eerie
Don't ask the driver to take the short cut|The short cut takes a year|A passenger asks a rickshaw driver to take the short cut; the ride lasts a year, and when he gets out his family has moved.|PASSENGER; DRIVER; WIFE|A rickshaw on a narrow lane|He wanted to save ten minutes|The driver has been taking this shortcut for thirty years|The rickshaw, the changed street, the passenger's old house|departure|sad
Never sit behind the driver alone|He has no reflection in the mirror|A woman sits behind a rickshaw driver at night; she notices his mirror shows the road, but not him.|WOMAN; DRIVER; THE MIRROR|A rickshaw on a dark road|It was the only rickshaw available|The driver died on this road and keeps driving|The mirror, the empty driver's seat, the moving rickshaw|reflection|eerie
Don't pay the driver with a note you found|The note is from his funeral|A man pays a rickshaw driver with a note he found on the road; the driver looks at it and says it's the money his family gave for his funeral.|MAN; DRIVER; THE NOTE|A rickshaw stand after a funeral|He had no change|The driver is dead and the note is his fare back|The note, the driver's hand, the empty rickshaw|debt|eerie
Never tell the driver your address twice|He takes you to the first one|A passenger gives his address twice because the driver didn't hear; the driver takes him to his childhood home, where his mother is still waiting.|PASSENGER; DRIVER; MOTHER|A rickshaw on a familiar road|He thought the driver was deaf|The first address is the house he left twenty years ago|The old house, the mother at the door, the rickshaw leaving|departure|sad
Don't leave your phone in a rickshaw|It calls you from the rickshaw|A man leaves his phone in a rickshaw; the phone calls him from the stand, and the driver says it has been ringing all night.|MAN; DRIVER; THE PHONE|A rickshaw stand after midnight|He was drunk|The caller is the next passenger, who found the phone and his address|The phone, the rickshaw, the man's own voice on the line|mimicry|eerie
Never ask a rickshaw driver his name|He gives you yours|A woman asks a rickshaw driver his name to make conversation; he says her name, and she can't remember her own.|WOMAN; DRIVER; HUSBAND|A rickshaw on a rainy street|She wanted to be friendly|The driver is the man who will drive her to the hospital where she dies|The rain, the driver's smile, the woman's blank face|mimicry|sad
Don't take a rickshaw with a broken meter|The meter counts something else|A man takes a rickshaw with a broken meter; the meter is counting his breaths, and the fare doubles every time he exhales.|MAN; DRIVER; THE METER|A rickshaw with a cracked meter|It was the only one at the stand|The meter is counting the years he has left|The meter, the man's breath, the driver's hand out|counting|eerie
Never let a rickshaw driver choose the music|The song knows your family|A driver plays a song the passenger has never heard; the lyrics include his mother's name and the date she died.|PASSENGER; DRIVER; THE RADIO|A rickshaw with a cassette player|He didn't want to argue|The song was recorded at his mother's funeral|The radio, the passenger's face, the driver humming|witness|sad
Don't take the last rickshaw at the stand|It was waiting for a funeral|A man takes the last rickshaw at the stand; the driver says he was about to leave for a funeral, and the man is sitting where the body should go.|MAN; DRIVER; THE BODY|A rickshaw stand at closing time|He was desperate|The funeral is the man's own, and the rickshaw is early|The rickshaw, the empty seat, the driver's white cloth|departure|eerie
# Crossings and signals
Never cross while the signal is blinking|The crossing counts your steps|A man crosses on a blinking signal; the crossing counts his steps, and the next day it starts counting his daughter's.|MAN; DAUGHTER; THE SIGNAL|A busy crossing with a broken signal|He was late for a meeting|The crossing is counting every member of his family|The signal, the daughter's shoes, the road|counting|eerie
Don't look at the driver who doesn't stop|He sees you for the rest of your life|A woman looks at a driver who runs a red light; he looks back, and for the rest of the week she sees him in every window.|WOMAN; DRIVER; HUSBAND|A crossing at rush hour|She wanted to shame him|The driver is the man who will cause her accident|The crossing, the driver's face in a window, the woman's hand|witness|eerie
Never cross where someone died|They cross with you|A boy crosses at a spot where a girl was killed; halfway across, he feels a small hand in his.|BOY; MOTHER; THE GIRL|A road with a faded memorial|He was taking a shortcut|The girl wants him to stay on the other side|The road, the small hand, the boy's empty sleeve|invitation|sad
Don't hold a stranger's hand at a crossing|They don't let go|An old man offers his hand to a woman at a busy crossing; she takes it, and he doesn't let go when they reach the other side.|OLD MAN; WOMAN; THE CROWD|A crowded crossing in the rain|She didn't want to be rude|The old man is her father, who died in this crossing years ago|The crossed road, the held hand, the woman's wedding ring|invitation|sad
Never cross with your eyes closed|You arrive on the other side of a different road|A girl crosses the road with her eyes closed for a dare; she opens them on a street she has never seen, and her house is gone.|GIRL; FRIEND; THE STREET|A residential crossing|She wanted to prove she was brave|The street is her street, but twenty years later|The unfamiliar house, the girl's school bag, the empty road|threshold|eerie
Don't wait for the green man at a broken signal|Someone else waits with you|A commuter waits at a broken signal; a man in a uniform waits beside him, and the signal only turns green when the man steps into the road.|COMMUTER; MAN IN UNIFORM; THE SIGNAL|A crossing with a broken pedestrian light|He didn't want to jaywalk|The man is a traffic officer who died at this crossing|The green light, the empty road, the officer's cap|threshold|eerie
Never cross a road you've dreamed about|The dream was a warning, and you're in it|A man crosses a road he saw in a nightmare; the cars pass through him, and the dream continues on the other side.|MAN; WIFE; THE DREAM|A road identical to his dream|He wanted to prove it was just a dream|The dream is the memory of his death, and he is repeating it|The road, the cars, the wife asleep in bed|witness|eerie
Don't step over a puddle at a crossing|The puddle is deeper than it looks|A woman steps over a puddle to keep her shoes clean; her foot goes through the road, and she stands in a flooded room below.|WOMAN; CHILD; THE ROOM|A crossing after rain|She didn't want to ruin her shoes|The room below is the house that was demolished to build the road|The puddle, the flooded room, the woman's clean shoes|threshold|eerie
Never cross after a funeral procession|The procession crosses back|A man crosses the road after a funeral procession; the procession returns, walking backward, and he is at the front.|MAN; MOURNER; THE PHOTO|A road outside a graveyard|He was in a hurry|The procession is taking him to the grave|The road, the mourners, the man's face in the photo|departure|eerie
Don't drop your keys at a crossing|Someone picks them up and uses them|A woman drops her keys at a crossing; a stranger picks them up, and that night her door opens with her keys still in her bag.|WOMAN; STRANGER; HUSBAND|A crossing outside her apartment|She was distracted|The stranger has been looking for a house like hers|The keys, the open door, the stranger's shoes|threshold|eerie
# Fog and night roads
Never drive into fog with your high beams on|The fog learns your face|A driver uses high beams in thick fog; for a second the fog shows a face in front of the car, and the face is his own.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE FACE|A highway covered in fog|He wanted to see better|The face is the driver from the car that died here|The fog, the headlights, the driver's face|witness|eerie
Don't stop for a figure waving on a foggy road|The figure is waving you back|A driver stops for a woman waving in the fog; she is waving him back toward a bridge that has collapsed.|DRIVER; WOMAN; THE BRIDGE|A foggy road near a bridge|He thought she needed help|The bridge collapsed years ago, and she is warning him|The fog, the bridge, the woman's hand|witness|sad
Never follow the tail lights in fog|They belong to a car that crashed|A driver follows tail lights through fog; the lights stop at a tree, and the car is empty and rusted.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE CAR|A mountain road in fog|He didn't want to get lost|The car crashed twenty years ago, and the driver is still following it|The rusted car, the tail lights, the fog|departure|eerie
Don't roll down the window to listen|The road hears you|A man rolls down his window to hear a sound on a quiet road; the road repeats his name in his mother's voice.|MAN; WIFE; THE ROAD|A deserted road at night|He thought he heard a child|The voice is coming from the road itself|The window, the empty road, the man's hand on the glass|mimicry|eerie
Never drive a road you don't know at night|The road knows you|A salesman takes an unknown road at night; every turn leads back to the same sign, and the sign has his address.|SALESMAN; TRUCK DRIVER; THE SIGN|A rural road with one sign|He wanted to avoid traffic|The road is a loop built around his house|The sign, the salesman's car, the same tree|threshold|eerie
Don't pick up a passenger in fog|They were already in the car|A driver picks up a passenger in fog; the passenger says he has been in the back seat since the driver left home.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; WIFE|A car on a foggy road|He didn't want to leave someone stranded|The passenger is the driver's younger self|The back seat, the driver's reflection, the fog|mimicry|eerie
Never turn off your headlights to see better|Something else turns them on|A driver turns off his headlights on a dark road to see a shape; the lights come back on by themselves, and the shape is in the passenger seat.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE SHAPE|A dark road with no streetlights|He thought the shape was a deer|The shape is the driver's dead brother|The dashboard lights, the empty road, the brother's hand|invitation|eerie
Don't honk at a shape in the fog|It honks back with your horn|A driver honks at a shape in the fog; the shape honks back, using the same horn, and the driver's horn stops working.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE SHAPE|A foggy road with a blind curve|He wanted to warn it|The shape is a car that crashed here, still trying to pass|The silent horn, the fog, the other car's lights|mimicry|eerie
Never drive past your destination twice|The third time, the road takes you|A man misses his turn twice; the third time, the road takes him to a version of his house where his family doesn't know him.|MAN; WIFE; THE HOUSE|A road outside his neighborhood|He was distracted|The house is his, but he died on this road years ago|The house, the unfamiliar family, the man's car|threshold|sad
Don't leave a light on in the back seat|Someone reads your mail|A driver leaves the back seat light on; in the mirror he sees a man reading his letters, and the letters are addressed to the man.|DRIVER; THE READER; WIFE|A car on a night road|He forgot to turn it off|The reader is the driver's father, who died before he was born|The mirror, the letters, the back seat|witness|sad
# Hitchhikers and strangers
Never pick up a hitchhiker in white|White is what they were buried in|A driver picks up a hitchhiker in white; the man asks to be dropped at a graveyard, and the gate is open.|DRIVER; HITCHHIKER; THE GATEKEEPER|A highway at dusk|He felt sorry for him|The hitchhiker died on this road and is going home|The white clothes, the graveyard, the empty car|departure|eerie
Don't stop for a child on the road at night|The child is older than you|A driver stops for a child standing on the road; the child asks for a lift to a village that was abandoned before the driver was born.|DRIVER; CHILD; THE ELDER|A road beside a sugarcane field|He didn't want to leave a child alone|The child is the last survivor of the abandoned village|The child, the dark field, the driver's headlights|witness|sad
Never give a stranger a lift to your own street|They know which house is yours|A driver gives a stranger a lift; the stranger asks to be dropped at the driver's street, and then at the driver's door.|DRIVER; STRANGER; WIFE|A car outside the driver's house|He was being polite|The stranger is the driver's future self, coming home to warn him|The street, the door, the driver's own face|mimicry|eerie
Don't ask a hitchhiker where they're going|They tell you where you're going|A driver asks a hitchhiker his destination; the hitchhiker says the driver's destination, and the driver can't remember his own.|DRIVER; HITCHHIKER; THE ROAD|A highway at night|He wanted to make conversation|The hitchhiker is going to the driver's funeral|The road, the hitchhiker's smile, the driver's hands|departure|eerie
Never let a stranger hold your phone|They call your mother|A driver lets a stranded stranger use his phone; the stranger calls the driver's mother and says goodbye.|DRIVER; STRANGER; MOTHER|A car at the side of the road|The stranger said his battery was dead|The stranger is the driver's brother, who died before the driver was born|The phone, the mother's voice, the stranger's hand|mimicry|sad
Don't share your water with a hitchhiker|They drink your voice|A driver shares his water with a hitchhiker; the hitchhiker drinks, and the driver can't speak for the rest of the trip.|DRIVER; HITCHHIKER; PASSENGER|A car on a hot road|The hitchhiker looked thirsty|The hitchhiker is the driver's voice, leaving his body|The bottle, the driver's open mouth, the silent radio|contamination|eerie
Never drop a stranger at a graveyard|They ask you to come in|A driver drops a stranger at a graveyard; the stranger invites him in, and the gate closes behind the car.|DRIVER; STRANGER; GATEKEEPER|A graveyard gate at midnight|He didn't want to be rude|The stranger is the driver's grandfather, waiting for a visit|The gate, the car, the grandfather's hand|invitation|sad
Don't take a stranger's bag into your car|The bag is heavier when they leave|A driver helps a stranger with a heavy bag; when the stranger gets out, the bag is still in the car, and it is heavier.|DRIVER; STRANGER; WIFE|A car at a bus station|He wanted to be helpful|The bag contains the driver's future|The bag, the driver's hands, the open road|debt|eerie
Never let a hitchhiker sit behind you|You see them in the mirror, but not the seat|A driver lets a hitchhiker sit in the back; in the mirror he sees the man, but when he turns, the seat is empty.|DRIVER; HITCHHIKER; PASSENGER|A car on a dark highway|The front seat was full|The hitchhiker is the driver's reflection, left behind|The mirror, the empty seat, the driver's face|reflection|eerie
Don't leave a stranger at a crossroads|The crossroads keeps them|A driver drops a stranger at a crossroads; the stranger stands there for years, and every car that passes sees him.|DRIVER; STRANGER; THE CROSSROADS|A crossroads with a dead tree|He was going the other way|The stranger is the driver's guilt, waiting for a lift|The crossroads, the waiting figure, the driver's car|witness|sad
# Petrol pumps
Never use the last pump at night|The pump counts your car|A driver uses the last pump at a deserted station; the pump counts his car, and every night it counts one more.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; THE PUMP|A petrol pump on a highway|The other pumps were closed|The extra car is the one that died at this pump|The pump, the attendant, the extra car|counting|eerie
Don't pay the attendant with exact change|He knows you've been there before|A driver pays with exact change; the attendant says he has been waiting for him since the last time, and the driver has never been here.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; WIFE|A petrol pump at dusk|He wanted to be quick|The driver died here in a previous life|The change, the attendant's smile, the pump|debt|eerie
Never use a petrol pump with no customers|The attendant has been waiting for you|A driver stops at an empty pump; the attendant says he was told to wait for a man with the driver's car.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; THE CAR|An empty petrol pump at night|He was low on fuel|The attendant is the driver's father, who died at this pump|The pump, the car, the father's hand|invitation|sad
Don't look at the attendant's face|He has yours|A driver avoids looking at the attendant's face; in the mirror, the attendant's face is the driver's, and it is smiling.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; MIRROR|A petrol pump with a broken light|He didn't want to be rude|The attendant is the driver's reflection, working the night shift|The mirror, the attendant's face, the driver's hands|reflection|eerie
Never fill up where the lights flicker|The fuel is from another car|A driver fills his tank at a pump with flickering lights; the car runs smoothly, but the fuel gauge goes up instead of down.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; PASSENGER|A roadside pump with flickering lights|It was the only station open|The fuel is from the car that crashed here|The gauge, the flickering light, the empty road|contamination|eerie
Don't use the bathroom at a petrol pump after midnight|The mirror remembers you|A driver uses a petrol pump bathroom at midnight; the mirror is fogged with a handprint, and the handprint is his.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; THE MIRROR|A petrol pump bathroom|He couldn't wait|The handprint is from the driver's last visit, which hasn't happened yet|The mirror, the handprint, the driver's hand|reflection|eerie
Never ask for a receipt|The receipt has your time of death|A driver asks for a receipt; the receipt prints a time, and the clock in his car stops at that exact time.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; THE CLOCK|A petrol pump counter|He needed it for expenses|The time is the driver's death, and he is already late|The receipt, the clock, the attendant's hand|witness|eerie
Don't leave your car running while you pay|Someone drives it away with you in it|A driver leaves his car running while he pays; he turns around and the car is driving away with him in the driver's seat.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; PASSENGER|A petrol pump at night|He was in a hurry|The driver in the car is the man who will die in it|The car, the driver's seat, the attendant waving|mimicry|eerie
Never buy anything from the pump shop|The shop sells what you lost|A driver buys a cold drink from a pump shop; the shopkeeper gives him a toy he lost as a child, and the toy is still warm.|DRIVER; SHOPKEEPER; MOTHER|A small shop beside a petrol pump|He was thirsty|The shop sells objects from the customer's childhood|The toy, the cold drink, the driver's hand|debt|sad
Don't drive away without checking the back seat|The attendant is there|A driver leaves a petrol pump without checking the back seat; at the next signal, the attendant asks him to pay for the fuel.|DRIVER; ATTENDANT; PASSENGER|A car on a highway|He was late|The attendant died at the pump and needs the fare|The back seat, the attendant's hand, the fuel gauge|debt|eerie
# Underpasses and bridges
Never walk under a bridge at night|The bridge remembers your footsteps|A man walks under a bridge at night; the bridge repeats his footsteps, then adds a second pair.|MAN; WIFE; THE BRIDGE|An underpass beneath a railway bridge|He was taking a shortcut|The second pair belongs to the man who died under the bridge|The bridge, the footsteps, the man's shadow|mimicry|eerie
Don't spit into a dry river|The river spits back|A boy spits into a dry riverbed; that night his mouth fills with mud, and he can't speak.|BOY; MOTHER; THE RIVER|A bridge over a dry river|He was playing|The river is returning everything it swallowed|The dry riverbed, the boy's mouth, the bridge|contamination|eerie
Never stop on a bridge to look down|Something looks up|A driver stops on a bridge to look at the river; a face looks up from the water, and it is his own.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE FACE|A bridge over a river at night|He wanted to see the view|The face is the driver from the car that went over the edge|The bridge, the water, the driver's face|reflection|eerie
Don't cross a new bridge alone|The bridge adds a step|A man crosses a new bridge alone; halfway across, the bridge adds a step, and the other side is farther than it was.|MAN; WIFE; THE BRIDGE|A newly built bridge over a canal|He wanted to test it|The bridge is measuring whether he is alone|The bridge, the extra step, the far bank|threshold|eerie
Never throw a coin into a river from a bridge|The coin comes back in your pocket|A woman throws a coin into a river for luck; the next day the coin is in her pocket, and it is warm.|WOMAN; HUSBAND; THE COIN|A bridge over a river|She wanted good luck|The coin was a payment for someone else's wish|The coin, the river, the woman's pocket|debt|eerie
Don't take a photo under an underpass|The photo has a car that isn't there|A boy takes a photo under an underpass; the photo shows a car behind him, but the road is empty.|BOY; FRIEND; THE CAR|An underpass with graffiti|He wanted a cool picture|The car is the one that will hit him|The photo, the empty underpass, the boy's smile|witness|eerie
Never walk through an underpass with headphones on|You miss the warning|A girl walks through an underpass with headphones on; she doesn't hear the man behind her, and she doesn't hear the bridge close.|GIRL; MAN; THE BRIDGE|A pedestrian underpass|She was listening to music|The man is her father, trying to warn her|The headphones, the closed underpass, the girl's hand|witness|sad
Don't follow the lights under a bridge|They lead to a room below|A man follows lights under a bridge; they lead to a room with a table set for dinner, and his family is waiting.|MAN; WIFE; THE FAMILY|Under a bridge at night|He thought they were rescue lights|The family is the one he lost in a flood|The table, the bridge above, the man's empty chair|invitation|sad
Never cross a bridge during a funeral|The bridge closes both ends|A man crosses a bridge during a funeral procession; the bridge closes at both ends, and the procession walks toward him.|MAN; MOURNERS; THE BRIDGE|A bridge near a graveyard|He was late for work|The funeral is his own, and the mourners are waiting|The bridge, the procession, the man's face|departure|eerie
Don't leave your shoes on a bridge|Someone walks home in them|A man leaves his shoes on a bridge to feel the water; he walks home barefoot, and his shoes are at his door in the morning.|MAN; WIFE; THE SHOES|A bridge over a canal|He wanted to cool his feet|The shoes walked home with someone else|The shoes, the door, the man's bare feet|contamination|eerie
# Last bus and train
Never take the last bus if it's empty|The bus is full|A man boards the last bus and finds it empty; halfway through the route, every seat is full, and the passengers are all facing the back.|MAN; CONDUCTOR; PASSENGERS|A last bus at midnight|He wanted to get home|The passengers are the ones who missed their stop|The bus, the silent passengers, the man's stop passing|invitation|eerie
Don't sit in the last row of the last bus|The row is for the driver's family|A woman sits in the last row of the last bus; the driver says the row is reserved, and his family is already sitting there.|WOMAN; DRIVER; FAMILY|The last bus of the night|It was the only seat|The driver's family died in a crash on this route|The last row, the empty seats, the driver's photo|invitation|sad
Never ask the conductor if this is the last bus|He says yes, and it is|A passenger asks the conductor if this is the last bus; the conductor says yes, and the bus stops at a depot that isn't on the route.|PASSENGER; CONDUCTOR; DRIVER|A bus at midnight|He wanted to be sure|The depot is a graveyard for buses and passengers|The depot, the old buses, the conductor's hand|departure|eerie
Don't sleep on the last bus|You wake at the depot, and the depot is a graveyard|A man sleeps on the last bus; he wakes in a depot full of buses with no drivers, and one bus has his name.|MAN; CONDUCTOR; THE DRIVER|A last bus at night|He was exhausted|The bus with his name is the one he will die in|The depot, the named bus, the man's reflection|departure|eerie
Never take the last train without a ticket|The ticket collector is dead|A man boards the last train without a ticket; the collector finds him, and the collector's uniform is from a funeral.|MAN; COLLECTOR; PASSENGER|A train platform at night|He thought no one would check|The collector died on this train and still collects fares|The ticket, the collector's hand, the empty carriage|debt|eerie
Don't get off at a station with no name|The station knows yours|A passenger gets off at an unnamed station; the sign changes to his name as he steps onto the platform.|PASSENGER; STATION MASTER; WIFE|A small station with no sign|He thought it was his stop|The station is the place where he will die|The sign, the platform, the passenger's bag|threshold|eerie
Never leave your luggage on the last bus|It arrives at your house before you|A woman leaves her bag on the last bus; when she gets home, the bag is on her doorstep, and the bus hasn't arrived at the depot yet.|WOMAN; CONDUCTOR; HUSBAND|A bus stop outside her house|She was tired|The bag was delivered by the passenger who died on the bus|The bag, the doorstep, the bus passing|departure|eerie
Don't trust the timetable on the last bus|The timetable is a list of names|A man reads the timetable on the last bus; the times are names, and his name is at the bottom.|MAN; CONDUCTOR; PASSENGER|A bus with a timetable card|He wanted to know the arrival time|The timetable lists everyone who died on the route|The card, the man's name, the dark window|witness|eerie
Never take the last bus twice|The second time, you're the driver|A man takes the last bus two nights in a row; the second night, the driver is gone and the man is in his seat.|MAN; DRIVER; CONDUCTOR|A last bus at midnight|He liked the quiet|The man is now the driver for the next passenger|The driver's seat, the man's hands, the empty road|mimicry|eerie
Don't wave at the last bus if it doesn't stop|It stops for you next time|A woman waves at the last bus, but it doesn't stop; the next night it stops, and the conductor opens the door for her alone.|WOMAN; CONDUCTOR; DRIVER|A bus stop after midnight|She was desperate to get home|The bus is coming back for her|The bus, the open door, the empty street|invitation|eerie
# Directions and maps
Never ask a stranger for directions after dark|They give you directions to their house|A man asks a stranger for directions; the stranger gives him a route that ends at a house with the stranger's name on the gate.|MAN; STRANGER; WIFE|A street corner at night|He was lost|The stranger's house is the man's childhood home|The gate, the nameplate, the man's key|invitation|eerie
Don't follow a map you found|The map is updating|A boy follows a map he finds in a library book; the streets change as he walks, and the map shows him getting closer to a red dot.|BOY; MOTHER; THE MAP|A city street with an old map|He wanted an adventure|The red dot is his house, and it is moving|The map, the red dot, the boy's street|threshold|eerie
Never take the shortest route through a graveyard|The route takes you|A man takes the shortest route through a graveyard; the path ends at his own grave, and the date is today.|MAN; WIFE; THE GRAVE|A graveyard at dusk|He was late for dinner|The grave is already dug|The grave, the man's name, the setting sun|departure|eerie
Don't trust a road that isn't on the map|The road knows you're lost|A driver takes a road that isn't on his map; every sign points to a town with his family's name.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE SIGN|A road through empty fields|He wanted to avoid a jam|The town is the one his grandparents left|The sign, the fields, the driver's family photo|witness|sad
Never turn left because a voice said so|The voice is your own, from later|A driver hears a voice tell him to turn left; he does, and the voice says, "Not yet."|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE VOICE|A car at a crossroads|He was curious|The voice is the driver's future self, trying to stop the crash|The crossroads, the driver's hands, the empty road|mimicry|eerie
Don't ask a child for directions|The child leads you to a house that's been demolished|A man asks a child for directions; the child leads him to an empty plot where a house used to be, and the child goes inside.|MAN; CHILD; THE HOUSE|A neighborhood street|He couldn't find the address|The child lived in the house before it was demolished|The empty plot, the child's hand, the man's car|invitation|sad
Never follow a kite string|The kite is tied to a grave|A boy follows a kite string across the rooftops; the string ends at a graveyard, tied to a child's grave.|BOY; MOTHER; THE KITE|A rooftop with a kite|He wanted the kite|The kite belongs to the child in the grave|The string, the grave, the kite in the sky|witness|sad
Don't take the road that smells of flowers|The flowers are from a funeral|A driver takes a road that smells of flowers; the road ends at a funeral procession, and the procession is waiting for him.|DRIVER; MOURNERS; THE FLOWERS|A road through a village|He liked the smell|The funeral is his own, and the flowers are from his garden|The road, the procession, the driver's car|departure|eerie
Never drive toward a light you can't identify|The light is your headlights from another car|A driver follows a light on a dark road; the light is his own headlights, coming toward him from the opposite direction.|DRIVER; PASSENGER; THE LIGHT|A dark highway|He thought it was a town|The other car is the driver's car, and he is in it|The light, the two cars, the driver's face|reflection|eerie
Don't stop to read a sign that wasn't there yesterday|The sign has your name|A driver stops to read a new sign; the sign is a missing-person poster with his photo and the date he disappeared.|DRIVER; WIFE; THE SIGN|A road with a new sign|He was curious|The sign was put up after he disappeared|The sign, the driver's photo, the empty road|witness|eerie
# Roadside stalls
Never eat at a stall with no customers|The food is for someone who died|A traveler eats at an empty roadside stall; the vendor says the food was ordered by a man who died on this road.|TRAVELER; VENDOR; THE MAN|A roadside stall with one bench|He was hungry|The man who ordered is the traveler, in a future he can't remember|The plate, the vendor's smile, the empty road|debt|eerie
Don't buy fruit from a cart at midnight|The fruit is from a graveyard|A woman buys fruit from a cart at midnight; the fruit is cold and smells of earth, and the cart is gone when she turns around.|WOMAN; CART SELLER; HUSBAND|A roadside fruit cart|She wanted something fresh|The fruit was left on a grave|The fruit, the empty road, the woman's hands|contamination|eerie
Never drink tea where the owner won't blink|The tea is his|A man drinks tea at a stall where the owner never blinks; the tea tastes of cardamom and ash, and the owner says it's his own.|MAN; OWNER; PASSENGER|A roadside tea stall|He was cold|The owner died at the stall and still serves tea|The cup, the owner's open eyes, the empty bench|debt|eerie
Don't ask the price of an unseen thing|The price is your name|A customer asks the price of a covered dish at a roadside stall; the vendor says his name, and the customer can't remember it.|CUSTOMER; VENDOR; FRIEND|A stall with a covered dish|He was curious|The dish contains the customer's name|The covered dish, the vendor's hand, the customer's face|mimicry|eerie
Never sit at a stall after the owner closes|The stall stays open for you|A man sits at a stall after the owner closes; the owner leaves, but the stall stays open, and a new owner serves him.|MAN; OWNER; NEW OWNER|A roadside stall at closing time|He wanted to finish his tea|The new owner is the man himself, years later|The stall, the new owner, the man's tea|mimicry|eerie
Don't take the last cigarette from a stall|It's the owner's last|A man takes the last cigarette from a roadside stall; the owner says it was his last, and he dies before the man lights it.|MAN; OWNER; PASSENGER|A cigarette stall by the road|He didn't know|The owner's last cigarette was a signal for his death|The cigarette, the owner's hand, the empty stall|debt|eerie
Never eat from a plate that's still warm|Someone is still eating|A traveler eats from a plate that is still warm; the vendor says the previous customer is still eating, and the chair is pulled out.|TRAVELER; VENDOR; THE CUSTOMER|A roadside dhaba|He was in a hurry|The previous customer is the traveler's dead brother|The plate, the pulled-out chair, the traveler's hand|invitation|sad
Don't pay with a coin that has no date|The coin is from his pocket|A customer pays with an old coin that has no date; the vendor says the coin is from his own pocket, and he gives it back.|CUSTOMER; VENDOR; THE COIN|A roadside stall at dusk|He found it on the road|The coin belongs to the vendor's dead son|The coin, the vendor's palm, the customer's change|debt|sad
Never ask a stall owner where he gets his food|He points to your house|A customer asks a stall owner where he gets his food; the owner points down the road, and the road leads to the customer's house.|CUSTOMER; OWNER; WIFE|A roadside stall|He was making conversation|The food comes from the customer's kitchen|The road, the customer's house, the owner's smile|witness|eerie
Don't leave a tip at a roadside stall|The tip is a debt|A man leaves a tip at a roadside stall; the next day the owner arrives at his house to return the favor.|MAN; OWNER; WIFE|A roadside stall|He was happy with the food|The owner brings a dish made from the man's favorite memory|The owner at the door, the covered dish, the man's wife|debt|eerie
